{#
calendar/_agenda_shell.html.twig — what an agenda LOOKS like, with nothing in it.

The third of the shells, after _month_shell and _time_grid_shell, and extracted
for the same reason: a day names itself, and whatever the caller draws goes
under it. The authenticated calendar puts chips there that open an editor; the
public shared page puts redacted rows there that have nothing to open. The day
heading — its date format, the way today is marked, the way it pins to the top
of the scroller while its own events are on screen — is the same object in
both, and a copy of it drifts on the first change to either.

**A day is a heading with its events under it, not a row with a date beside
it.** The rail this shell started with — a fixed 44px column carrying weekday
over number over month — did line the entries up, and paid for it twice: it
spent that width on every row of a pane that is narrow by design, and it left
the date reading as a label ON the first event rather than as a heading over
all of them. A heading that is `sticky top-0` names the day for as long as any
of its events is on screen, which is the thing a rail cannot do at any width,
and hands the whole column back to the entries.

What lines the entries up now is a time lane inside the row — "all day", or a
start over an end — which both callers here already wanted: sharing/_entry_row
has printed one since before this shell existed, and _event_chip grew
`timeColumn` to match it.

**The lane is as wide as the widest time in the WHOLE list and not a pixel
more, and that is the one thing a fixed width could not be.** It was `w-16`, a
number picked so a translated "all day" would fit: 64px, against 33 that a
"10:00" actually wants, on every row of every day whether any of them said "all
day" or not. And it was still wrong in the other direction — en_PI's "all the
day long" wrapped to two lines inside it.

So the list is ONE grid rather than a stack of rows, and the lane is a
`fit-content(5rem)` track of it. A track is sized against every cell in its
column, which is exactly the question being asked, and `fit-content` caps a
pathological translation at 7rem rather than letting one row set the width of a
thousand — 7 because the shared page writes its range on one line and a
twelve-hour "6:00 pm–7:00 pm" wants nearly all of it. A cap only caps: the
owner's stacked "10:00" still measures 33px and pays nothing for the headroom. The day heading spans all three tracks; the day groups are
`display: contents` so they add no box between the rows and the tracks they
have to land on; and each row is `grid-template-columns: subgrid`, which is what
lets a row keep being a real box — a focusable <button>, a <li> — instead of
having to be flattened into the grid with `display: contents` as well.

CSS only, deliberately. A width measured in JavaScript would have to be
remeasured on every navigation, every pane drag and every font swap, and would
be wrong for the first frame of each.

── Whether an empty day is printed is the caller's decision ─────────────────

$showEmptyDays is false for the authenticated agenda: the value there is
density, it is the docked pane's default view, and a fortnight of "nothing"
between two events is noise — the grids are where an empty day still needs to
occupy its place.

It is true for the shared calendar, and that is not an inconsistency. A shared
page is read by somebody deciding whether to ask its owner for an hour, so
"free on the 4th" has to be a thing the page SAYS rather than a gap the reader
has to notice and interpret. The property is older than this shell — it is what
the shared page's day list did before the page had views at all — and it is
carried here deliberately rather than lost in the move.

── A day the caller is not publishing ───────────────────────────────────────

Same contract as the other two shells: a $dayKey with no entry in $days is a day
the caller is not publishing, which is NOT the same as a day with nothing on it.
It is left out of the list entirely — there is no honest way to draw "I am not
telling you about this day" as a row in a list of days, and the shared page
names the state in a legend under the list instead.

Embed it, do not include it:

    {% embed 'calendar/_agenda_shell.html.twig' with { ... } %}
        {% block day_entries %}…{% endblock %}
    {% endembed %}

Variables:
  dayKeys        list<string>  the days in range, Y-m-d, in order
  days           map           keyed by those; a MISSING key means the day is
                               outside the range the caller is publishing
  today          string        Y-m-d in the view's own zone, never the reader's
  showEmptyDays  bool          optional, default false

Blocks, rendered inside the day loop and able to read `dayKey` and `dayEntries`:
  day_entries  what the day holds
  day_empty    what a day with nothing on it says; only reachable when
               showEmptyDays is true
  empty_state  drawn instead of the whole list when it would have no rows
#}

{% set showEmptyDays = showEmptyDays|default(false) %}

{# Which days get a row, resolved before the list is opened rather than counted
   as it is drawn: the empty state is a decision about the WHOLE list, and a
   template that discovers it was empty after printing the container has already
   printed a container. #}
{% set agendaRows = [] %}
{% for dayKey in dayKeys %}
    {% if days[dayKey] is defined and (showEmptyDays or days[dayKey]|length > 0) %}
        {% set agendaRows = agendaRows|merge([dayKey]) %}
    {% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% if agendaRows is empty %}
    {% block empty_state %}{% endblock %}
{% else %}
    {# `minmax(0, 1fr)` on the title rather than `1fr`, for the reason the time
       grid gives at greater length: a bare `1fr` floors at its content's
       min-width, so one long unbroken word would push the list wider than the
       pane. The 4px mark gets `max-content`, which is the only honest way to
       say "as wide as it is". #}
    <div data-calendar-agenda class="grid gap-x-2"
         style="grid-template-columns: fit-content(7rem) max-content minmax(0, 1fr)">
        {% for dayKey in agendaRows %}
            {% set dayEntries = days[dayKey] %}
            {% set isToday = dayKey == today %}

            {# `contents`, so the heading and the rows below are children of the
               grid rather than of this. The element stays for `data-day`, which
               is what the shared page's tests ask a day by. #}
            <div class="contents" data-day="{{ dayKey }}">
                {# One ICU skeleton rather than a weekday and a date composed
                   here: German writes "Dienstag, 15. September" and English
                   "Tuesday, 15 September", and the field order is the locale's
                   to decide — see LocaleDateExtension, which is the whole
                   argument for the filter.

                   `top-0` because nothing is pinned above this. The agenda's
                   scroller is the pane's own, unlike the time grid's, which
                   stacks two rows of its own inside it and has to count them. #}
                <div class="col-span-full sticky top-0 z-10 flex items-baseline gap-2
                            border-y border-line px-3 py-1
                            {{ isToday ? 'bg-accent/10' : 'bg-sunken' }}">
                    <span class="text-xs font-medium {{ isToday ? 'text-accent' : 'text-ink' }}">
                        {{ dayKey|intl_date('EEEEdMMMM', false) }}
                    </span>
                </div>

                {% if dayEntries|length > 0 %}
                    {# The rows' own grid: it spans the three tracks and adopts
                       them, so what a caller puts inside lands on the same
                       columns every other day's rows do. `px-3` here rather
                       than on each row, which would mean each row shrinking
                       the first and last track by its own padding. #}
                    <div class="col-span-full grid grid-cols-subgrid px-3 py-1">
                        {% block day_entries %}{% endblock %}
                    </div>
                {% else %}
                    <div class="col-span-full px-3 py-1">
                        {% block day_empty %}{% endblock %}
                    </div>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
{% endif %}
